  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Polymeric Sands", link: "/best-polymeric-sand/" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">Troubleshooting</span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">Field Tested</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">Removing <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Polymeric Sand Haze</span></h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          That white film is glue, not dust — which is exactly why sweeping it, hosing it and waiting it out have all failed.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">Start Here</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">Jump to the FAQ</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto"><SocialProof users="6.8k" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">In This Guide</h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What the Haze Actually Is</a>
          <a href="#timing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How Long You Have</a>
          <a href="#escalation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Escalation Order</a>
          <a href="#paver-type" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. It Depends on Your Pavers</a>
          <a href="#pressure" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Pressure Washing Safely</a>
          <a href="#not-haze" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. When It Is Not Haze</a>
          <a href="#prevention" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Preventing It Next Time</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The joints set perfectly, which was the point. The problem is that a thin skim of the same binder set on top of the pavers at the same moment, and it is every bit as durable there as it is in the joints.
        </p>

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What the Haze Actually Is</h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand is graded sand mixed with a polymer binder. Sweep it into the joints, add water in the specified way, and the binder activates and cures, locking the sand into a semi-flexible mass that resists washout, weeds and ants.
          </p>

          <p>
            The binder cannot tell the difference between sand in a joint and sand dust sitting on a paver face. Concrete pavers have a texture that holds fine dust tenaciously — a broom never gets it all, and the residue is invisible when dry. Add water and that residue cures into a translucent, slightly milky film bonded to the surface.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Why Ordinary Cleaning Fails</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You are not cleaning dust off a surface. You are trying to remove a cured adhesive that has bonded to it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Sweeping does nothing because there is nothing loose. Soap and water does nothing because the polymer is not water-soluble once cured. This is why people scrub for an hour and see no change at all, and conclude the pavers are permanently ruined. They are not — the approach is just wrong.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The visual signature is a uniform milky or greyish veil across the paver faces, usually more obvious when the surface is dry and often almost invisible when wet. It sits over the whole installed area rather than concentrating anywhere, which distinguishes it from efflorescence, and it feels smooth rather than powdery, which distinguishes it from unactivated sand dust.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Long You Have</h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time Since Install</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Polymer State</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What Works</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Set but green</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stiff brush and a firm hose rinse</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1-7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hardening</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brush plus a haze remover</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1 week - 3 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fully cured</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated remover or dilute acid</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 3 months</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fully cured and weathered</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chemical plus mechanical, repeated</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The first row is worth acting on urgently if you are reading this the morning after an installation. A stiff-bristled push broom worked hard across a wet surface, followed by a thorough hose rinse, lifts a great deal of green haze in twenty minutes. That same haze at a fortnight will take a bottle of chemical and an afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution on brushing early: work along the joints rather than across them, and keep the water off the joints as much as you reasonably can. The joint material is also still curing, and flooding it or scrubbing across it can pull sand out and create the washout problem covered in <a href="/why-is-my-polymeric-sand-washing-out/">why polymeric sand washes out</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="escalation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Escalation Order</h2>

          <p>
            Start mild. Every step up carries more risk to the paver face and to the joints you just installed, so work upward only as far as you need to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step One: Stiff Brush and Water</h3>
          <p>
            A stiff nylon or poly push broom, the surface kept wet, worked hard in the direction of the joints. On light or recent haze this alone can be enough. Rinse thoroughly afterwards so the loosened material does not simply dry back onto the surface, and let everything dry before judging the result — remember the haze is far less visible wet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step Two: A Dedicated Polymeric Haze Remover</h3>
          <p>
            Most polymeric sand manufacturers sell one, and they are formulated for exactly this film on exactly these surfaces. They are the right second move because they are considerably gentler on joint material than a general-purpose masonry acid. Follow the dwell time on the label, keep the surface from drying, agitate with a brush, and rinse thoroughly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step Three: Dilute Acidic Masonry Cleaner</h3>
          <p>
            On acid-tolerant concrete pavers only. A masonry cleaner based on phosphoric or sulfamic acid, diluted per the label, softens the cured polymer so it can be brushed away. Wet the pavers first so the acid does not soak straight into dry pores, work in small sections, never let it dry, and rinse extremely thoroughly — residual acid continues attacking joint material long after you have finished.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step Four: Controlled Pressure Washing</h3>
          <p>
            The last resort, covered in its own section below, because it removes haze and joint sand with equal enthusiasm.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Test Every Product in a Hidden Area</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Behind a planter, under a step, at the edge nearest the house — somewhere you can live with a bad result. Apply, dwell, rinse and let it dry fully before judging, because everything looks fixed while wet. Acids can lighten or etch some concrete pavers, and the only way to know how yours respond is on a square you are not looking at every day.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="paver-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. It Depends Heavily on Your Pavers</h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Standard concrete pavers</strong> are the most forgiving. They tolerate dilute acidic cleaners, they take moderate brushing without damage, and their texture — while it is what trapped the dust in the first place — is durable. Most successful haze removals are on these.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Coloured or face-mix concrete pavers</strong> need more care, because the colour is concentrated at the surface. Aggressive acid or hard mechanical work can lighten the face and leave a patch that is more obvious than the haze was. Use the mildest thing that works and keep the treatment uniform across the whole area rather than spot-treating.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Natural stone — limestone, travertine, marble</strong> — must never see acid of any kind, vinegar included. These are calcareous stones and acid dissolves them, etching the surface permanently. Use only a pH-neutral haze remover specified as safe for natural stone, and accept that mechanical work and patience are your main tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bluestone, granite and slate</strong> generally tolerate mild acids but vary by quarry and finish. Test conservatively. Honed and polished finishes are far more vulnerable to visible etching than a natural cleft or flamed surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not certain what your pavers are, treat them as acid-sensitive until a hidden test proves otherwise. Etching is permanent, and haze is not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pressure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Pressure Washing, If It Comes to That</h2>

          <p>
            Pressure will remove cured polymer. It will also remove the joint sand you installed, etch concrete paver faces, and drive water into the base — which is why it sits at the bottom of the escalation order rather than the top.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you use one, keep it to 1,200 to 1,500 psi with a 25 or 40 degree tip held at least twelve inches back, and work parallel to the joints rather than across them so the spray does not blast directly into the sand. Keep the wand moving constantly and never dwell, because a moment of hesitation leaves a visibly cleaner stripe you will then have to match across the whole patio.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan for joint repair afterwards. Expect to lose some sand depth, and top the joints up and re-activate once the pavers are dry, following the installation method properly this time. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">using polymeric sand</a> and <a href="/polymeric-sand-installation-mistakes/">the common installation mistakes</a> cover doing that without repeating the haze.
          </p>

          <p>
            A surface cleaner attachment is meaningfully safer than a wand here, because it holds a fixed standoff and moves the spray in a controlled arc, so the pressure never dwells in one spot. If you own or can rent one, it is the better tool for a patio-sized area.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-haze" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When It Is Not Actually Haze</h2>

          <p>
            Two other white problems get confused with polymeric haze, and they need entirely different treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Efflorescence</strong> is salt deposited from within the paver as moisture moves through it. It looks powdery or crystalline rather than filmy, it feels gritty, it concentrates in patches rather than covering evenly, and it often appears weeks or months after installation rather than immediately. New concrete pavers commonly efflorece in their first year regardless of what you did with sand. It responds to efflorescence cleaners, and it frequently returns until the pavers finish curing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Unactivated sand dust</strong> is simply dust that never got wet. It brushes and rinses off easily, which is the immediate test — if a wet brush lifts it in seconds, that is what you have and you are fortunate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The distinguishing test between haze and efflorescence is a wet finger. Wet the area: haze largely disappears while wet and returns as the surface dries, because the film's refractive index changes. Efflorescence stays visible wet and often looks slightly worse. Related diagnosis is in <a href="/why-paver-sealer-turns-white/">why paver sealer turns white</a>, which covers the third variant where a sealer has been applied over either problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Preventing It Next Time</h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Use a leaf blower, not just a broom.</strong> Held at a low angle after sweeping, it clears the fine dust a broom leaves sitting in the paver texture. This single step prevents most haze.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Work on bone-dry pavers.</strong> Dew is enough to start setting dust. Wait until the surface is genuinely dry before sweeping sand in, and never install on a damp morning.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Blow the surface again immediately before watering.</strong> Sand migrates back onto the faces while you work. The last thing you do before the hose comes out should be a final blow-off.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Water with a shower setting, not a jet.</strong> A hard stream splashes sand slurry up onto the paver faces, which is haze being created in front of you.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Do a section at a time on a large area.</strong> Sweep, blow and water one manageable zone before moving on, rather than filling the whole patio and then watering it all.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Check the surface at a low angle in raking light.</strong> Dust that is invisible looking straight down is obvious from a crouch with the sun behind it.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Should You Bother At All?</h2>
        <p>
          It is worth asking honestly, because removal is not free and not risk-free. A very light haze on a textured concrete paver, in a garden setting seen mostly from a distance, will weather down over a season or two and may never be worth a bottle of acid and the risk of etching a face. If you only notice it when you are looking for it, that is a reasonable case for leaving it alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cases that genuinely justify the work are a heavy, obviously milky film; a formal patio or entrance seen close up every day; any surface you intend to seal, since sealing locks the haze in permanently; and darker pavers, where a pale veil sits in much sharper contrast than it does on buff or grey. In those situations the haze does not weather out fast enough to wait for, and dealing with it while it is only a few weeks old is far easier than tackling it a year later.
        </p>

        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating It as Dust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sweeping and soapy water do nothing, because cured polymer is not loose and is not water-soluble. An hour of scrubbing with no change convinces people the pavers are ruined. They are not — the film needs chemistry or abrasion, not effort.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Acid on Natural Stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Limestone, travertine and marble dissolve in acid, vinegar included, and the etching is permanent and far more visible than the haze. If you cannot identify your pavers with certainty, treat them as acid-sensitive until a hidden test proves otherwise.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going Straight to the Pressure Washer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It removes haze and joint sand with equal enthusiasm, and can etch paver faces. Work up through brushing and chemical removers first, and if you do reach for pressure, use a surface cleaner rather than a wand and plan to top the joints up afterwards.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A sealer locks the haze in permanently, and on a wet-look product the surrounding gloss makes the trapped film more obvious rather than less. Remove the haze, let the pavers dry fully, and seal afterwards if you still want to.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the Result While Wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Haze largely vanishes on a wet surface and returns as it dries. Every treatment looks like a total success for the first twenty minutes. Let the area dry completely before deciding whether to escalate, or you will stop too early or go too far.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is polymeric sand haze?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">A film of cured polymer binder left on the paver surface. When the joints were activated with water, fine sand dust still on the pavers got wet too and its binder set into a hard translucent film. It is not dust and not efflorescence — it is the same adhesive holding your joints together.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Within a day or two, a stiff brush and firm rinse often lift it while the polymer is green. Once cured you need a dedicated haze remover, a diluted acidic masonry cleaner on acid-tolerant pavers, or careful mechanical removal. Test everything in a hidden area first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will it go away on its own?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Partly, over a season or two, as UV and traffic wear a light film away. A heavy haze will not disappear and often becomes more visible as the pavers around it weather and it does not. If it bothers you now, it will still bother you in six months.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pressure wash it off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Carefully, as a last resort. Pressure lifts cured polymer but also strips joint sand and can etch paver faces. Stay at 1,200 to 1,500 psi with a 25 degree tip a foot back, work parallel to the joints, and expect to top up the sand afterwards.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinegar work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Weakly, and only on light haze — acetic acid is far milder than a formulated masonry cleaner. Worth trying on acid-tolerant concrete pavers before escalating. Never use it on natural stone, since it etches limestone, travertine and marble permanently.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you avoid it next time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Get every trace of sand off the paver faces before adding water. Sweep the joints, sweep the surface, then use a leaf blower at a low angle to clear what the broom left in the texture. Work on bone-dry pavers, and never activate on a damp surface.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal over haze?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">You can, and it is a poor idea. Sealing locks the haze in permanently, and on a wet-look sealer the trapped film often becomes more visible because the gloss around it raises the contrast. Remove it, dry the pavers, then seal.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do you have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">The easy window is the first 24 to 48 hours while the polymer is set but not hardened — a brush and a rinse often take it off. After a week it needs chemical or mechanical removal. It never becomes impossible, just progressively more work.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

