        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">The haze is cured polymer, not dust</strong> — which is why sweeping and washing achieve nothing. Fine sand left on the paver faces got wet along with the joints and set into a film bonded to the surface.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Act fast if you can. Within 24 to 48 hours a stiff brush and a firm rinse often lift it. After that, escalate slowly: a dedicated haze remover first, then a dilute acidic masonry cleaner on acid-tolerant concrete pavers only, and pressure washing last because it strips joint sand too.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Never put acid on limestone, travertine or marble, always test in a hidden area, and always judge the result dry rather than wet. Next time, a leaf blower over bone-dry pavers immediately before watering prevents the whole problem.
          </p>
        </div>

